          %h2#ideas Ideas
          %h3#general General
          %p
            Quite generally, the
            %em completion of a ring
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/completion"} completion
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+ring"} topological ring
            to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+space"} complete
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+ring"} topological ring
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normed+ring"} normed ring
            to a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+ring"} Banach ring
          %h3#formaladic_completion_and_formal_neighbourhoods Formal/adic completion and formal neighbourhoods
          %p
            A special case of ring completion is the
            %em formal completion
            or
            %em adic completion
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adic+topology"} adic topology
            induced by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maximal+ideal"} maximal ideal
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I\subset R
            (
            %a{:href => "#Sullivan05"}> Sullivan 05, definition 1.3
            ).
          %p
            The underlying ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi R
                      %mo ^
                    %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat R_I
            of this formal completion is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi R
                      %mo ^
                    %mi I
                  %mo ≔
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %mo ←
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\widehat R_I \coloneqq \underset{\leftarrow}{\lim} (R/I^n)
          %p
            (formed in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CRing"} CRing
            of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+rings"} commutative rings
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotients"} quotients
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            by all the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/powers"} powers
            of this ideal,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            (
            %a{:href => "#Sullivan05"}> Sullivan 05, proposition 1.13
            ). Notice that this may be considered purely algebraically.
          %p
            In words, this limit construction says that the elements of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_I
            are sequences of elements in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            which “successively add smaller and smaller elements, as seen by the ideal
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            rings, which are indeed the archetypical example of formal completions, see example
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FormalPowerSeries"}
            below.
          %p
            Generally, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isbell+duality"} dual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometric
            meaning of formal ring completion is in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+geometry"} formal geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum+%28geometry%29"} geometric spectrum
            of a formally completed ring is known as a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+spectrum"} formal spectrum
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Spf
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mo ,
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spf(R,I)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+neighbourhood"} formal neighbourhood
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum+of+a+commutative+ring"} spectrum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec(R/I)
            inside
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Spec
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec(R)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi Spf
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi R
                      %mo ^
                    %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Spec(R/I)
                  \\hookrightarrow
                  Spf(\widehat R_I)
                  \\hookrightarrow
                  Spec(R)
                  \\,.
          %h3#derived_completion_functor Derived completion functor
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functor"} derived functor
            of adic completion was originally discussed in (
            %a{:href => "#GreenleesMay92"} Greenless-May 92
            (“Greenlees-May duality”). For discussion of its relation to derived
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsion+subgroup"} torsion subgroup
            functor see (
            %a{:href => "#PortaShaulYekutieli10"}> Porta-Shaul-Yekutieli 10
            ) and see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/fracture+theorem#CompletionAndTorsionOnDerivedCategories"} fracture theorem – Arithmetic fracturing for chain complexes
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %p
            The archetypical example which most clearly exhibits the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometric
            meaning of formal completions of rings is the following
          #FormalPowerSeries.num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R[x]
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial+ring"} polynomial ring
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R[x]
              at the ideal
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x)
              generated by the free generator
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              is the ring of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R[ [x] ]
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Spec
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec(R[x])
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+line"} affine line
              in
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+geometry"} arithmetic geometry
              over
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Spf
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spf(R[ [x] ])
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+disk"} formal disk
              inside the affine line around the origin.
          %p More generally:
          %p
          #CompletionOfNoetherianRingByPowerSeries.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/completion+of+a+ring"} completion
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Noetherian+ring"} Noetherian ring
                by
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+series"} power series
              %br/
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Noetherian+ring"} Noetherian ring
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I = (a_1, \cdots, a_n)
              a finitely generated
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ideal"} ideal
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              at
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
              to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+series+ring"} power series ring
              over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              on the
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_i
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo
                          %mi I
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≃
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    R\hat{_{I}}
                    \\;\simeq\;
                    R
                    [ [ x_1, \cdots, x_n  ] ]
                    \/
                    \\big(
                    x_1 - a_1, \cdots, x_n - a_n
                    \\big)
                    \\,.
            %p
          %p
            (e.g.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/The+Stacks+Project"} The Stacks Project
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0316"} Lemma 0316
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "Atiyah-Segal+completion+theorem#Buchholtz08"} Buchholtz 08, Sec. 6.4
          %p
            The key class of example of completions in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-archimedean+analytic+geometry"} non-archimedean analytic geometry
            is the following.
          #pAdicNumbers.num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-adic+integers"} p-adic integers
              are the completion of the ring of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
              at the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prime+ideal"} prime ideal
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi ℤ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p) \subset \mathbb{Z}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-adic+rational+numbers"} p-adic rational numbers
              are the completion of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-adic+complex+numbers"} p-adic complex numbers
              are the completion of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
              at
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p)
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              In view of the example
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FormalPowerSeries"}
              one sees that the
              = succeed "-adic" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#pAdicNumbers"}
              are in fact
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analogy"} analogous
              to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
              rings, hence that they behave like
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+rings"} function rings
              on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+disks"} formal disks
              in
              %em some
              kind of geometry.
            %p
              This
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analogy"} analogy
              is part of what is known as the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+field+analogy"} function field analogy
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
              behaves like the would-be “ring of polynomials
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔽
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{F}_1[x]
              over
              = succeed "\u201D." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F1"} F1
          #AtiyahSegalTheorem.num_example
            %h6#example_3 Example
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Atiyah-Segal+completion+theorem"} Atiyah-Segal completion theorem
              states that for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+group"} topological group
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-space"} G-space
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
              ring
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⫽
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(X\sslash G)
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+quotient"} homotopy quotient
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⫽
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \sslash G
              (of the
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+construction"} Borel construction
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+K-theory"} equivariant K-theory
              ring of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %p
              So this says that the “very naive” equivariant K-theory embodied by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⫽
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(X  \sslash G)
              is an infinitesimal approximation to the genuine equivariant K-theory.
          .num_example
            %h6#example_4 Example
            %p
              The phenonemon of example
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AtiyahSegalTheorem"}
              appears for other
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology+theories"} generalized cohomology theories
              too, such as
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+cobordism"} complex cobordism
              (
              %a{:href => "#GreenMay97"}> GreenleesMay 97
              ). For
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+oriented+cohomology+theories"} complex oriented cohomology theories
              it says that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+group"} formal group
              assigned by these to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                    %mo ⫽
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast  \sslash U(1)
              is to be thought of as the formal completion of a more globally defined something. One case where this “something” has been understood in some detail is that where the cohomology theory is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+cohomology"} elliptic cohomology
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+elliptic+cohomology"} equivariant elliptic cohomology
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#RelationToTorsionApproximation Relation of formal completion to torsion approximation
          %p
            For suitable ideals
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔞
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}\subset A
            of a commutative ring
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functor"} derived functor
            of
            = succeed "-adic" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/completion+of+a+module"} completion of A-modules
            forms together with
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsion+approximation"} torsion approximation
            an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+modality"} adjoint modality
            on the
            %br/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+modules"} (∞,1)-category of modules
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %em
              %a{:href => "fracture+theorem#CompletionAndTorsionOnDerivedCategories"} arithmetic fracturing for chain complexes
            for details.
